== Lecture 10 · Building limits and understanding colimits
#focus[Construct limits from products and equalizers, dualize to colimits, and recognize quotient constructions as coequalizers.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall small indexing categories and the equivalence relation generated by a set of pairs.


#theorem[Products and equalizers construct limits][A category with all small products and equalizers has all small limits. Finite products and equalizers suffice for finite limits.]
#strategy[Put all candidate coordinates in one product and impose all compatibility conditions with one equalizer.]
#proof[For $D:J -> cal(C)$, form $P=product_(j in J)D j$ and $Q=product_(a:i -> j)D j$. Define $s,t:P -> Q$ by the components $s_a=D(a)pi_i$ and $t_a=pi_j$. Let $e:L -> P$ equalize $s,t$. Its composites with the product projections form a cone. Any cone defines a unique map $X -> P$, and its cone equations say this map equalizes $s,t$, so it factors uniquely through $L$. Uniqueness follows successively from equalizer and product uniqueness. If the indexing category has finitely many objects and arrows, the products needed are finite.]

#definition[Colimit][A cocone consists of maps $i_j:D j -> C$ with $i_j D(a)=i_i$ for $a:i -> j$. A colimit is a cocone through which every other cocone factors uniquely by an arrow from its apex. It is a limit in the opposite category.]

In sets, a colimit of a small diagram is obtained from the disjoint union of all its sets by identifying each element with its image under every diagram arrow, then taking the equivalence relation generated by those identifications. A cocone is exactly a function on the disjoint union that is constant on these equivalence classes, which proves the quotient's universal property.

#example[Coequalizers and pushouts][The coequalizer of $f,g:A -> B$ in sets is the quotient of $B$ by the equivalence relation generated by $f(a)~g(a)$. A pushout of $A <- C -> B$ is the tagged disjoint union of $A,B$ with the images of each $c$ identified. Taking only the listed pairs without reflexive, symmetric, and transitive closure generally fails.]

#theorem[Coproducts and coequalizers construct colimits][A category with all small coproducts and coequalizers has all small colimits.]
#proof[Apply the preceding limit theorem to the opposite category. Explicitly, the two arrows from the coproduct over diagram arrows to the coproduct over objects encode respectively the source element and its image. Their coequalizer enforces exactly the cocone equations.]

#example[Computing a pushout of vector spaces][For $f:U->V$ and $g:U->W$, form $(V oplus W) slash N$, where $N$ is the image of $u mapsto(f(u),-g(u))$. The two summand maps become equal on the images of $U$. Given linear maps $a:V->X$, $b:W->X$ with $a f=b g$, the sum map $(v,w)mapsto a(v)+b(w)$ kills $N$ and therefore factors through the quotient. Every quotient element has a representative pair, so the factor is unique.]

This calculation is the linear counterpart of gluing tagged sets, but it uses a subspace of relations rather than just an equivalence relation on points. Substituting the set construction directly into vector spaces would miss the requirement that all linear consequences of the relations vanish.

#checkpoint("5.2", [In vector spaces, what is the coequalizer of $f,g:V -> W$?], [It is $W slash "im"(f-g)$ with the quotient map. A linear map equalizes $f,g$ exactly when it kills this image.])

